# ABI i386 calling: registers, stack at a call, parameters and return values
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Source: the i386 psABI, "Low Level System Information", Function Calling
# Sequence (tables fig-reg-usage and fig_returning_locations, the stack-frame
# section and "Returning Values in Memory"), compared with the older
# published revision (abi386-4, chapter 3, "Function Calling Sequence"). The
# rules apply to global functions only; every function called here is one.
# The 16-byte stack alignment at a call is the current revision's; the older
# one asked for word (4-byte) alignment, so that assertion is disputed and a
# weaker one (calling.010) holds for both. Where the ABI leaves bits
# undefined (the upper bits of a char or short return value) the test only
# checks that the compiler's own caller does not depend on them.

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.001
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       A call to strlen returns with EBX, ESI, EDI and EBP holding what the caller
           put there.
note       Both revisions (the older one also lists ESP). Sentinel values are loaded by
           a hand-written caller.
test       t/001-callee-saved-strlen.c
nomutant   the calls are to a C library; a planted fault would be the library's, and the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh with deliberately wrong routines instead

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.002
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       A call to memcpy returns with EBX, ESI, EDI and EBP holding what the caller
           put there.
note       Both revisions (the older one also lists ESP). Sentinel values are loaded by
           a hand-written caller.
test       t/002-callee-saved-memcpy.c
nomutant   the calls are to a C library; a planted fault would be the library's, and the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh with deliberately wrong routines instead

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.003
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       A call to qsort and its callback returns with EBX, ESI, EDI and EBP holding
           what the caller put there.
note       Both revisions (the older one also lists ESP). Sentinel values are loaded by
           a hand-written caller.
test       t/003-callee-saved-qsort.c
nomutant   the calls are to a C library; a planted fault would be the library's, and the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh with deliberately wrong routines instead

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.004
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       A call to snprintf with floating-point arguments returns with EBX, ESI, EDI
           and EBP holding what the caller put there.
note       Both revisions (the older one also lists ESP). Sentinel values are loaded by
           a hand-written caller.
test       t/004-callee-saved-snprintf.c
nomutant   the calls are to a C library; a planted fault would be the library's, and the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh with deliberately wrong routines instead

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.005
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       The direction flag is clear on return from every function, so after strlen,
           memcpy, qsort and snprintf when it was clear at the call.
note       Both revisions.
test       t/005-df-clear-on-return.c
nomutant   the calls are to a C library; a planted fault would be the library's, and the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh with deliberately wrong routines instead

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.006
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       The x87 control word is callee-saved: after strlen, memcpy, qsort and
           snprintf it is what it was before the call, even when it held a non-default
           value.
note       Current revision only (the older one gives no rule). The word is set to
           round-down before each call.
test       t/006-x87-cw-callee-saved.c
nomutant   the calls are to a C library; a planted fault would be the library's, and the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh with deliberately wrong routines instead

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.007
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       The MXCSR control bits are callee-saved (the status bits are not): after
           strlen, memcpy, qsort and snprintf they are what they were before the call,
           even when they held a non-default value.
note       Current revision only. MXCSR is set to round-down before each call. Needs a
           processor with SSE.
test       t/007-mxcsr-control-callee-saved.c
nomutant   the calls are to a C library; a planted fault would be the library's, and the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh with deliberately wrong routines instead

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.008
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      disputed
says       A function that returns no floating-point value leaves the x87 register
           stack empty, so the processor is in x87 mode for its caller.
note       Stated outright by the older revision (empty before entry and upon exit).
           The current revision asks for x87 mode at entry and for MMX users to clear
           their state before returning, which an empty stack satisfies; so a FAIL is
           INSPECT.
test       t/008-x87-empty-on-return.c
nomutant   the calls are to a C library; a planted fault would be the library's, and the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh with deliberately wrong routines instead

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.009
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       The direction flag is clear at every function entry, including a callback
           that a library function calls.
note       Both revisions. The callee is entered through a recording trampoline.
test       t/009-callback-df-clear.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.010
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/The Stack Frame (sec-stack-frame)
class      required
says       The stack is at least word aligned at a function entry: ESP + 4 is a
           multiple of 4 whoever made the call.
note       The part of the rule that holds in both revisions (the older says word
           aligned; the current says 16, see calling.011).
test       t/010-entry-word-aligned.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.011
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/The Stack Frame (sec-stack-frame)
class      disputed
says       At a function entry the value of ESP plus 4 is a multiple of 16, whatever
           the number and size of the arguments and the size of the caller's frame.
note       Follows the current revision (16-byte alignment). The older revision asks
           only for word alignment (calling.010), so a FAIL is INSPECT. Checks the
           compiler's calls.
test       t/011-entry-16-compiled-calls.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.012
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/The Stack Frame (sec-stack-frame)
class      disputed
says       A comparison function that qsort or bsearch calls is entered with ESP plus 4
           a multiple of 16.
note       Follows the current revision (16-byte alignment); the older revision asks
           only for word alignment, so a FAIL is INSPECT. Checks the library's calls.
test       t/012-entry-16-library-callbacks.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.013
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Parameter Passing and Returning Values/Passing Parameters
class      disputed
says       Arguments are placed on the stack in order starting at the stack pointer
           (the last one farthest away), each at its type's alignment and rounded up to
           whole words: an int, float or char takes a word, a double or long long two,
           a long double three, a 5-byte structure two.
note       The expansion of a char or short to a word and the rounding of a structure
           to whole words are stated by the older revision only; the current text asks
           for padding that enforces the alignments of its table, which for a char
           followed by a short leaves them packed, so a FAIL is INSPECT. The upper bits
           of a char or short argument word are not checked. The stack alignment at the
           call is calling.011, not this assertion.
test       t/013-stack-argument-layout.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.014
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values in Memory
class      required
says       A function returning a structure of any size, even one that would fit in a
           register, receives the address of the result as an implicit first argument,
           writes the value there, pops that argument and returns the address in EAX.
note       Both revisions agree on the hidden pointer and on EAX. In the current text
           all aggregates go to memory.
test       t/014-small-aggregates-in-memory.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.015
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values in Memory
class      required
says       A function returning a larger structure (12, 16, 100 and 33 bytes, one
           holding a long double) follows the same protocol: hidden first argument,
           value written there, argument popped, address in EAX.
note       Both revisions.
test       t/015-large-aggregates-in-memory.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.016
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations)
class      required
says       A function returning _Complex double or _Complex long double (types the
           table gives 'memory' as return location) uses the hidden-pointer protocol.
note       Current revision only.
test       t/016-complex-in-memory.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.017
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations)
class      disputed
says       A function returning float leaves exactly one value on the x87 register
           stack, in st0, holding that value; the caller removes it.
note       Disputed: the value in st0 is shared, but that it is the only one on the
           x87 stack (st1-st7 empty on exit) is stated by the older revision alone, so
           a FAIL is INSPECT. The record pops the value itself, as the caller must.
test       t/017-return-float.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.018
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations)
class      disputed
says       A function returning double leaves exactly one value on the x87 register
           stack, in st0, holding that value; the caller removes it.
note       Disputed: the value in st0 is shared, but that it is the only one on the
           x87 stack (st1-st7 empty on exit) is stated by the older revision alone, so
           a FAIL is INSPECT. The record pops the value itself, as the caller must.
test       t/018-return-double.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.019
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations)
class      disputed
says       A function returning long double leaves exactly one value on the x87
           register stack, in st0, holding that value; the caller removes it.
note       Disputed: the value in st0 is shared, but that it is the only one on the
           x87 stack (st1-st7 empty on exit) is stated by the older revision alone, so
           a FAIL is INSPECT. The record pops the value itself, as the caller must.
test       t/019-return-long-double.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.020
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (floating-point paragraph)
class      required
says       A caller pops a floating-point return value from the x87 register stack
           whether or not it uses the value.
note       Both revisions. The calls are made by the compiler's own code through
           prototyped function pointers.
test       t/020-unused-fp-return-popped.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.021
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations)
class      required
says       A function returning a 64-bit integer puts the high 32 bits in EDX and the
           low 32 bits in EAX.
note       Current revision only (the older one has no long long).
test       t/021-return-64-bit.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.022
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations)
class      required
says       A function returning an int, long, unsigned, enumeration, object pointer or
           function pointer leaves the value in EAX.
note       Both revisions.
test       t/022-return-eax.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.023
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations) (after the table, _Bool paragraph)
class      required
says       A function returning a _Bool leaves the truth value in bit 0 of AL and zero
           in bits 1 to 7.
note       Current revision only. The other bits of EAX are not checked.
test       t/023-return-bool.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.024
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations)
class      required
says       For a char or short return value only AL or AX is defined; the upper bits of
           EAX are undefined and the caller must not depend on them, so a signed char
           holding 0x80 in AL reads as -128 and an unsigned one as 128 whatever the
           upper 24 bits hold.
note       Current revision only. The callee is hand-written (garbage in the upper
           bits); what is checked is the compiled caller.
test       t/024-char-short-return-upper-bits.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.025
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (table fig_returning_locations)
class      disputed
says       A function returning _Complex float returns the real part in EAX and the
           imaginary part in EDX (the 32-bit patterns of the two floats).
note       The registers EAX and EDX are the current revision's (the older one has no
           _Complex). The check that no value is left on the x87 stack rests on the
           older revision alone, so a FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/025-return-complex-float.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code and of the library's entry points, not of one library function; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh


assertion  ABI.i386.calling.026
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       A function compiled from C, with many values live across calls to a
           function it cannot see into, returns with EBX, ESI, EDI and EBP holding
           what the caller put there.
note       Both revisions. The function asks for optimisation through a compiler
           attribute, so the driver needs no per-test flags; a compiler without the
           attribute builds it as it is. A conforming compiler never lets the
           callee-saved registers change, whatever it uses them for.
test       t/026-callee-saved-compiled.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code; the detector is validated by abi/control.sh with a compiler told to treat those registers as call-clobbered

assertion  ABI.i386.calling.027
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Returning Values (after table fig_returning_locations, _Bool paragraph)
class      required
says       A _Bool passed as an argument has the truth value in bit 0 and zero in
           bits 1 to 7 of its low byte, also when it was converted from a value that
           is nonzero and even.
note       Current revision only. The upper 24 bits of the word are not checked. The
           return value is calling.023. The callee reads the words as plain unsigned
           values; the caller is compiled code.
test       t/027-bool-argument.c
nomutant   a property of the compiler's generated code; the check applies to the compiler's own calls
